1 SET
    The SET command is used to set the state of information in the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  See the help text on:

    CONTROL     DEBUG       DEFAULT     ON
    PREFIX      PROMPT      QUANTUM     RADIX
    VERIFY      WORKING_SET

    A symbol is set with DCL's assignments (see HELP SYMBOL).

    The settings of the machine itself (breakpoints, stepping, the PSL, page
    table entries, the base address, the display modes) are the debugger's:
    see HELP SET in the debugger (type DEBUG, then HELP SET).
2 ASSEMBLER
    The SET ASSEMBLER command is used to control characteristics of the
    built-in instruction assembler in the console.

    Syntax:

        SET ASSEMBLER <flag>

    Parameters:


        [NO]ADDRESS_PROMPT

            When set, this flag tells the pseudo-assembler to display
            the memory address in which the instruction(s) will be
            written as part of the prompt for input.

        [NO]FORWARD_WARNINGS

            When set, this flag tells the assembler to issue a warning
            whenever the assembler finishes processing statements and
            there are unresolved forward references.  These symbols
            must be resolved before the console will allow a RUN
            statement to execute.

        [NO]BRANCH_DEST

            When set, disassembly of instructions will include a
            comment field that lists the numeric destinations of
            symbolic addresses.  This can make reading the disassembly
            somewhat easier.

        [NO]SYMBOLS

            When set, the disassembler will attempt to convert branch
            and address references back into symbolic names if there
            is a label or entry with a matching value.

        [NO]RESOLVE_TEMP

            When set, the assembler allows symbols prefixed with a "_"
            character to be considered "temporary" with the definition
            of a .ENTRY procedure.  When the end of the entry is
            assembled, the temporary names are qualified by the name of
            the entry that contains them, making them globally unique.

        [NO]SCOPENAMES

            When set, temporary symbols (prefixed with "_") are made
            global by qualifying them with the name of the ENTRY that
            contains them.  When clear, a unique integer number is
            generated instead to define the scope.  This makes the name
            of the symbol shorter (and easier to read in disassembly)
            but less clear which entry it belongs to.
2 CONTROL
    The SET NOCONTROL=Y command turns CTRL/Y off: pressing it echoes
    *Interrupt* and does nothing more, and CTRL/C does nothing to a
    program that hasn't enabled a CTRL/C AST.  It also cancels the
    current command level's ON CONTROL_Y.  SET CONTROL=Y turns CTRL/Y
    back on, with its default action.  The setting applies at every
    command level.  T (CTRL/T) is accepted and remembered, as
    F$ENVIRONMENT("CONTROL") reports it, but govax shows no statistics
    for it.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]CONTROL[=(T,Y)]

    With neither T nor Y, Y is meant.

    Example:

        $ SET NOCONTROL=Y
        $ SET CONTROL=Y         ! CTRL/Y's default action is back
2 DEBUG
    The SET DEBUG command sets various debugging switches in the console
    or emulator.  To set the debug switch, use it's name shown below.  To
    turn off the switch, previx the name with NO.

    Syntax:

        SET DEBUG [NO]<name>

    Parameter:

        <name>   The name of a debugging switch.  The values currently
                 supported are:

                 DEBUG         Switch to native debugger if available.

                 VM            Enable debugging messages for virtual memory
                               translations.

                 TB            Enable debugging messages for translation
                               buffer caching.

                 SYMBOLS       Enable debugging messages for symbol
                               creation or resolution.

                 REGISTERS     Enable display of registers that change
                               value on a STEP command.

                 INTERRUPTS    Enable debugging messages for interrupts.

                 EXCEPTIONS    Enable debugging messages for exceptions.

                 IMAGES        Enabled SYS$IMGACT mapping when RUN
                               command issued for an image.

                 USERHALT      Enable HALT instruction in USER mode.

                 KEYBOARD      Debug console keyboard IO activity.

                 SERVICES      Debug P1 vector calls to VMS system services.

                 RMS           Show each RMS service's FAB or RAB as the
                               service left it: the file and default
                               names, FAC, record format, and IFI; or the
                               stream (file, terminal, indirect), record
                               access mode, record number, the record,
                               and the status by name.

                 DCL           Debug DCL command line parsing in console.

                 CHM           Enable debug messages on CHMx or REI.

                 COMMAND       Display command line expansions after symbol
                               substitution in the console.

                 LOGICALS      Debug emulated VMS logical name support.

                 DEVICES       Debug emulated VMS device support.

                 LIBINIT       Enable LIB$INITIALIZE calls on image load.
2 DEFAULT
    The SET DEFAULT command establishes the operator's current default
    device and directory, the same way a partial file specification is
    resolved in real VMS DCL.  DIRECTORY, DELETE, PURGE, COPY, and TYPE
    all fill in any part of a file specification left unspecified (device,
    directory, or both) from this default.

    Syntax:

        SET DEFAULT <spec>

    Parameter:

        <spec>          A device and/or directory specification, such as
                        DUA0:[MYDIR].  Any part omitted (e.g. just
                        [MYDIR], or just DUA0:) is filled in from the
                        *current* default, the same way a real VMS
                        SET DEFAULT works.

    Logical names in <spec> are translated.  The device goes into the
    logical name SYS$DISK, which is how every file command (and a program's
    SYS$OPEN and SYS$CREATE) finds the default device; a concealed logical
    name is kept as the device rather than what it hides.  If <spec> starts
    with a search list, SYS$DISK is set to the search list's name and each
    element is searched in turn.

    Example:

        SET DEFAULT DUA0:[MYDIR]

    See also the help for SHOW DEFAULT.
2 EDIT
    The SET EDITOR command says which host editor EDIT runs, and where its
    files go.  The values are the configuration keys vax.editor.command and
    vax.editor.scratch, saved when govax exits, so later sessions keep them.

    Syntax:

         SET EDITOR [/COMMAND="host command"] [/SCRATCH="host directory"]

    Qualifiers:

         /COMMAND     The command to run.  %file in it stands for the file
                      to edit; without one, the file goes last.  It is split
                      into words at blanks, and quotes keep blanks in a word.
                      No shell runs it.  Quote the value, as DCL makes text
                      without quotes uppercase.  The default is "vi %file".

         /SCRATCH     An existing directory to work in.  EDIT makes a private
                      directory in it for each file.  The default is the
                      host's temporary directory.

    An empty value ("") goes back to the default.

    Examples:

         SET EDITOR/COMMAND="vim -n %file"

         SET EDITOR/COMMAND="code --wait"/SCRATCH="/tmp"
2 NOCONTROL
    The SET NOCONTROL=Y command turns CTRL/Y off: pressing it echoes
    *Interrupt* and does nothing more, and CTRL/C does nothing to a
    program that hasn't enabled a CTRL/C AST.  It also cancels the
    current command level's ON CONTROL_Y.  SET CONTROL=Y turns CTRL/Y
    back on, with its default action.  The setting applies at every
    command level.  T (CTRL/T) is accepted and remembered, as
    F$ENVIRONMENT("CONTROL") reports it, but govax shows no statistics
    for it.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]CONTROL[=(T,Y)]

    With neither T nor Y, Y is meant.

    Example:

        $ SET NOCONTROL=Y
        $ SET CONTROL=Y         ! CTRL/Y's default action is back
2 NOON
    The SET NOON command makes the command procedure it is in go on
    whatever its commands' statuses: no failure ends it, and ON's command
    isn't run.  $STATUS and $SEVERITY are still set.  SET ON turns the
    checking back on, with the action that was in effect before.  Each
    command level has its own setting; at the terminal, SET [NO]ON does
    nothing.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]ON
2 NOPREFIX
    The SET PREFIX command puts a prefix before each verified command line
    (see HELP SET VERIFY), made from a $FAO control string: the time each
    command runs, with "(!5%T) ".  The lines that continue a command get
    blanks as long as the prefix, and data lines get none.  The only
    directives that may take an argument are the time's and date's, !%T
    and !%D, which show the current time; a control string with another
    is refused (%DCL-E-INVPREFIX), and the prefix stays as it was.
    F$ENVIRONMENT("VERIFY_PREFIX") returns the control string; SET
    NOPREFIX takes it away.

    Syntax:

        SET PREFIX "control-string"
        SET NOPREFIX

    Example:

        $ SET PREFIX "(!5%T) "
        $ SET VERIFY
        $ @TEST
        (17:52) $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN
2 NOVERIFY
    The SET VERIFY command shows each line of a command procedure (@) as it
    runs: procedure verification shows its command lines, and image
    verification its data lines, as an image (or TYPE SYS$INPUT), the
    debugger, or the assembler reads each one.
    SET VERIFY and SET NOVERIFY turn both on or off; a keyword, which may
    be abbreviated, changes only its own (SET NOVERIFY takes none).  A
    change lasts after the procedure that made it ends.

    A command line is shown as it is in the file, its "$", indentation,
    label, and comment included, after 'SYMBOL' substitution (&SYMBOL and
    the symbols of an expression are not shown replaced).  Comment lines
    are shown too, and a continued command a line at a time, each line
    substituted.  The lines of an IF branch that doesn't run are not
    shown (its ELSE or ENDIF is), nor is the THEN of an IF that was false,
    an ON command's action, or a command typed at the terminal.

    F$VERIFY (see HELP LEXICAL) reads and sets both settings, and DCL
    performs it even in a comment, between apostrophes, showing the
    comment with its value in place of the call: a procedure that starts
    with

        $ ! 'F$VERIFY(0)'

    turns verification off without that line being shown.

    Syntax:

        SET VERIFY [=([NO]PROCEDURE, [NO]IMAGE)]
        SET NOVERIFY

    Examples:

        SET VERIFY

        SET VERIFY=PROCEDURE

        SET VERIFY=(NOPROCEDURE, IMAGE)

        SET NOVERIFY
2 ON
    The SET NOON command makes the command procedure it is in go on
    whatever its commands' statuses: no failure ends it, and ON's command
    isn't run.  $STATUS and $SEVERITY are still set.  SET ON turns the
    checking back on, with the action that was in effect before.  Each
    command level has its own setting; at the terminal, SET [NO]ON does
    nothing.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]ON
2 PREFIX
    The SET PREFIX command puts a prefix before each verified command line
    (see HELP SET VERIFY), made from a $FAO control string: the time each
    command runs, with "(!5%T) ".  The lines that continue a command get
    blanks as long as the prefix, and data lines get none.  The only
    directives that may take an argument are the time's and date's, !%T
    and !%D, which show the current time; a control string with another
    is refused (%DCL-E-INVPREFIX), and the prefix stays as it was.
    F$ENVIRONMENT("VERIFY_PREFIX") returns the control string; SET
    NOPREFIX takes it away.

    Syntax:

        SET PREFIX "control-string"
        SET NOPREFIX

    Example:

        $ SET PREFIX "(!5%T) "
        $ SET VERIFY
        $ @TEST
        (17:52) $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN
2 PROMPT
    The SET PROMPT command changes the prompt the console shows when it
    reads a command.  The new prompt is also stored as the configuration
    value vax.console.prompt, so it stays in effect in later govax
    sessions.

    Syntax:

        SET PROMPT="<text>"

    Parameter:

        <text>          The new prompt, in double quotes so that its case
                        and any blanks (such as a trailing one) are kept.
                        An empty string ("") returns to the default
                        prompt, "VAX> ".

    The ASM> prompt of the interactive assembler and the debugger's DBG>
    prompt are not changed.

    Examples:

        SET PROMPT="vax$ "

        SET PROMPT=""
2 QUANTUM
    The SET QUANTUM command sets the internal interrupt interval value,
    expressed as the number of instrutions that must pass before an
    interrupt or process context switch can occur. Defaults to 1. Change
    this only if you need to debug interrupt and context switching operations.
2 RADIX
    The SET RADIX command is used to set the default radix (numeric base) of
    constant values that do not have an explicit radix indicator like X^ or
    D^.

    Syntax:

        SET RADIX <base>

    Parameter:

        <base>          The specified radix.  Valid values are HEXADECIMAL
                        or DECIMAL.

    Examples:

        SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL       ; constant will be interpreted as hex
                                    ; numeric values as if the X^ prefix
                                    ; had been used.
2 VERIFY
    The SET VERIFY command shows each line of a command procedure (@) as it
    runs: procedure verification shows its command lines, and image
    verification its data lines, as an image (or TYPE SYS$INPUT), the
    debugger, or the assembler reads each one.
    SET VERIFY and SET NOVERIFY turn both on or off; a keyword, which may
    be abbreviated, changes only its own (SET NOVERIFY takes none).  A
    change lasts after the procedure that made it ends.

    A command line is shown as it is in the file, its "$", indentation,
    label, and comment included, after 'SYMBOL' substitution (&SYMBOL and
    the symbols of an expression are not shown replaced).  Comment lines
    are shown too, and a continued command a line at a time, each line
    substituted.  The lines of an IF branch that doesn't run are not
    shown (its ELSE or ENDIF is), nor is the THEN of an IF that was false,
    an ON command's action, or a command typed at the terminal.

    F$VERIFY (see HELP LEXICAL) reads and sets both settings, and DCL
